From: gt510 <gt510@localhost>
Subject: aperture: scale the V4L2 encoder bitrate to the camera's real frame rate

The camera source fixates 30 fps (aperture caps the camera caps at
MAXIMUM_RATE and pipewiresrc picks the maximum), and V4L2 encoders such
as the Venus encoder budget every frame for the frame rate in the caps.
A camera behind a software ISP delivers fewer frames: the SM-T550 rear
camera gives ~24 fps on USB power and ~8.5 on battery, so a recording got
80 % (or 30 %) of DEFAULT_BITRATE: 1.65 Mbit/s for 2 Mbit/s.

Keep the timestamps of the camera's last 30 frames (buffer probe on the
camera source pad) and, before each recording, raise the bitrate of a
v4l2h264enc in camerabin by the ratio of the negotiated to the measured
frame rate (1x to 4x). Declaring the real rate in the caps instead does
not work: everything downstream is negotiated for the fixated rate.
